{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/body",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "body"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "The biological, embodied, and socially interpreted dimension of a person through which individuals experience the world, participate in social relations, and are classified and evaluated."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "It also carries cultural meanings related to sex, femininity, ability, attractiveness, sexuality, and independence. In this sense, bodily characteristics are socially interpreted as markers of sex and as a basis for expectations directed at girls and women, while disability may modify or challenge normative representations of the female body (This approach is consistent with the CRPD perspective, which distinguishes between sex and gender and highlights their interconnections with disability and discrimination). The body can be analysed through bodily characteristics and functions, the experience of one’s own body, its social evaluation and regulation, norms concerning appearance and femininity, experiences of sexuality and reproduction, as well as barriers and practices affecting bodily autonomy."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "7.2"
  ],
  "broader": {
    "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7",
    "prefLabel": {
      "en": "Gender, sex and sexuality"
    }
  },
